  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of early this evening, a cold front is about\n150 miles offshore of ENC. This front has ushered in strong CAA\nand ensuing gusty nrly winds. There continues to be high \nconfidence that a strong Nor'easter will form well off the \nCarolina coast through the latter half of the weekend into early\nto mid next week. Strong winds, high waves, and coastal \nflooding as well as extremely dangerous marine conditions will \nbe the result. \n\nThere is still model uncertainty beyond early week on where the\nlow pressure area will be, but the trend in 17/12Z global\nguidance including ensemble packet has shifted a bit further\noffshore. Nevertheless, this does Not diminish any of the\nadvertised high coastal impacts, as long duration and long fetch\nnnerly winds will bring large waves and pile up water along the\ncoast, leading to coastal flooding well into the week. \n\nHigh winds on the coast still expected. Have fine-tuned the wind\nadv, taking W Carteret out but keeping the OBX and nern zones\ncloser to the stronger gradient winds in tact. High wind warning\ncriteria being met appear to be lessening, though can't rule out\na few gusts reaching 60 mph early this evening with the initial\nCAA surge. With the further offshore track during the week, this\nshould also keep winds lower.\n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Impacts are expected to \nboth sound and oceanside locales with potentially significant \nimpacts to vulnerable areas on the ocean side. A mix of coastal \nflood headlines is in effect to cover these hazards. One recent\naddition to the suite of coastal headlines was to issue a\nCoastal Flood Advisory for Tyrrell County. The Sheriff's office\nreported HWY 94 is flooded, with additional public reports of\ndeep water along that stretch of highway. This is likely due to\nwater piling up on the southern end of the Alligator River with\nstrong, gale-force winds blowing straight south down the length\nof the river.\n\nAdditionally, a highsurf advisory is in effect for the Outer \nBanks with breaking waves of generally 8-14 feet expected in the\nsurf zone. A moderate to high risk of rip currents is also \nexpected through much of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After record breaking heat the last several \ndays, temperatures are expected to cool significantly. Maximum \ntemperatures through the period are expected to remain in the \n60-70 degree range with cooler values inland and warmer \ntemperatures near the coast. Overnight lows will range from the \n40s inland just after the frontal passage to the 50s by the end\nof the week with coastal locations in the 50s early in the \nforecast period to near 60  by the end of next week. Winds will\nremain breezy, particularly in the afternoons depending upon \nthe eventual track of the offshore nor'easter. \n\nRegarding rain, main batch should end by this evening as area of\nfgen/lift shifts offshore. As the low meanders offshore, some\nwrap around rain may ocnl sneak back onshore, with best chances\non the OBX over the next several days.",
